Q14. Implement MapReduce Operation in MongoDB

```javascript
use CollegeDB

db.Students.insertMany([
    {Student_ID: 201, Name: "Yash", Course: "BCA", Marks: 85},
    {Student_ID: 202, Name: "Rahul", Course: "BCA", Marks: 78},
    {Student_ID: 203, Name: "Aman", Course: "BBA", Marks: 82},
    {Student_ID: 204, Name: "Priya", Course: "MCA", Marks: 91},
    {Student_ID: 205, Name: "Neha", Course: "BCA", Marks: 88}
])

var mapFunction = function() {
    emit(this.Course, {
        count: 1,
        total: this.Marks
    });
};

var reduceFunction = function(course, values) {
    var result = {
        count: 0,
        total: 0
    };

    values.forEach(function(value) {
        result.count += value.count;
        result.total += value.total;
    });

    return result;
};

db.Students.mapReduce(
    mapFunction,
    reduceFunction,
    {
        out: "CourseResults"
    }
);

db.CourseResults.find();

db.CourseResults.find().forEach(function(doc) {
    print(
        "Course: " + doc._id +
        " Students: " + doc.value.count +
        " Average Marks: " +
        (doc.value.total / doc.value.count)
    );
});
```

Q15. Study Database Distribution Models

1. Single Server

Client
|
Database Server
|
Database

Data Distribution:
All data is stored on one server.

Failure Handling:
If the server fails, the database becomes unavailable.

Advantage:
Easy to manage.

Limitation:
Limited scalability.

Application:
Small College Database.

### 2. Sharding

Client
|
MongoDB Router
|          |
Shard 1    Shard 2

Data Distribution:
Data is divided across multiple shards.

Failure Handling:
Unavailable shards can interrupt access unless replication is configured.

Advantage:
Handles large datasets.

Limitation:
Complex configuration.

Application:
E-Commerce Website.

### 3. Master-Slave Replication

Client
|
Primary Server
|          |
Secondary  Secondary

Data Distribution:
Primary copies data to secondary servers.

Failure Handling:
An eligible secondary can become primary through replica set election.

Advantage:
High availability.

Limitation:
More storage is required.

Application:
Banking Database.

### 4. Peer-to-Peer Replication

Node 1
|       |
Node 2 - Node 3

Data Distribution:
Multiple nodes participate in reading and writing data.

Failure Handling:
Other nodes continue working when one node fails, depending on replication settings.

Advantage:
No single primary server.

Limitation:
Consistency management is complex.

Application:
Distributed IoT Systems.

### 5. Sharding and Replication

Client
|
MongoDB Router
|             |
Shard 1       Shard 2
|             |
Primary       Primary
|             |
Secondary     Secondary

Data Distribution:
Data is divided into shards and each shard has replica servers.

Failure Handling:
Replica servers provide availability if a primary server fails.

Advantage:
Scalability and fault tolerance.

Limitation:
High setup and maintenance cost.

Application:
Large E-Commerce Platforms.
